  "historical_output_text": " I was always the type to overcomplicate things. One day, I decided to bake a cake for the first time. I meticulously followed a recipe I found online, measuring each ingredient with precision. But as I was folding in the frosting, I accidentally knocked a few sprinkles onto the counter. I panicked, thinking the whole cake was ruined. My anxiety only grew as I stared at the mess. \n\nBut then I took a deep breath and looked at the cake. It was still perfectly fine. Just a few stray sprinkles, hardly noticeable. I realized my overreaction was the real problem, not the minor mistake. I carefully picked up the sprinkles and smoothed the frosting out a bit. The cake turned out great, and it tasted even better knowing I had overcome my perfectionism.\n\nSince that day, I've tried to remember to take things like that in stride. Mistakes happen, but they're usually not as big of a deal as I make them out to be. It's a small lesson, but one that's stuck with me and helped me in other areas of life too. Sometimes the smallest adjustments can make the biggest difference. I'm still",
